# Fault Tolerance

Fault tolerance is the ability of a system to continue operating correctly even when one or more components fail.

The goal is not to prevent failures (failures are inevitable), but to keep the service available despite them.

## Simple Example

Imagine you are driving a car.

- If one headlight stops working and the car still runs safely, the car is fault tolerant.
- If the engine stops because one headlight failed, the car is not fault tolerant.

Similarly in software, if one server crashes but users can still use the application, the system is fault tolerant.

## Why Fault Tolerance Matters

In distributed systems, failures happen all the time:

- Servers crash
- Networks fail
- Hard disks die
- Databases become unavailable
- Entire data centers can go offline

Without fault tolerance:

- Users cannot access the application
- Data may be lost
- Revenue and trust are impacted

## Example 1: Web Application

Suppose your application has only one server:

```text
Users
      |
Web Server
```

If the server crashes, the website is down.

Now add a load balancer and two servers:

```text
                                     +------------+
Users -----> | Load Balancer
                                     +------------+
                                           /        \
                              Server A    Server B
```

If Server A crashes:

```text
                                     +------------+
Users -----> | Load Balancer
                                     +------------+
                                                            |
                                                 Server B
```

Traffic is routed to Server B, and users often do not notice. That is fault tolerance.

## Example 2: Database Replication

Single database setup:

```text
Application
            |
 Database
```

If the database fails, everything stops.

Replicated setup:

```text
                        Primary DB
                                    |
                  Replication
                                    |
                   Secondary DB
```

If primary fails:

```text
Application
            |
Secondary DB (promoted)
```

The secondary becomes the new primary. Data loss is zero or minimal, depending on replication mode.

## Example 3: F5 BIG-IP HA

This is a practical high-availability pattern:

```text
Client
      |
Active BIG-IP
      |
Servers

Standby BIG-IP
```

The standby continuously receives config/state updates and monitors the active device.

If active BIG-IP fails:

```text
Client
      |
Standby becomes Active
      |
Servers
```

Traffic management and application delivery continue with minimal interruption.

## Common Fault Tolerance Techniques

### 1. Redundancy

Keep multiple copies of components.

```text
Server A
Server B
Server C
```

If one fails, others continue serving traffic.

### 2. Replication

Maintain multiple copies of data.

```text
Primary Database
                  |
       Replica 1
                  |
       Replica 2
```

### 3. Failover

Automatically switch to a backup component.

```text
Primary Server
                  |
       Failure
                  |
Backup Server
```

### 4. Load Balancing

Distribute requests across multiple instances.

```text
Users
      |
Load Balancer
      |
Server1 Server2 Server3
```

If one server dies, traffic is sent to healthy instances.

### 5. Health Checks

Continuously test whether components are alive.

Example:

- Every 5 seconds: Are you alive?
- Yes -> keep in rotation
- No -> remove from traffic

### 6. Retry Mechanism

Temporary failures often self-resolve.

```text
API Call -> Failed -> Retry -> Success
```

Many cloud systems apply retries with backoff automatically.

### 7. Circuit Breaker Pattern

If a downstream service keeps failing, stop sending traffic to it temporarily.

Without circuit breaker:

```text
App -> Failed Service
App -> Failed Service
App -> Failed Service
```

With circuit breaker:

```text
App -> Circuit Open -> Return Error Fast
```

After a cooldown, the system probes the dependency again.

### 8. Data Backups

If a full data store is lost, backups allow restoration.

## Fault Tolerance vs High Availability

These terms are related but not identical.

| Fault Tolerance | High Availability |
|---|---|
| Continues operating despite failures | Minimizes downtime |
| Often no visible interruption | May have brief interruption during failover |
| Requires more redundancy and cost | Usually easier and less expensive |
| Example: multiple active servers | Example: active-standby BIG-IP |

## Real-World Examples

- Netflix: if one microservice or availability zone fails, traffic is rerouted and streaming continues.
- Google Search: requests are served from multiple data centers, so one outage does not stop service.
- Banking systems: replicated databases reduce transaction data loss risk.
- Amazon: if one server fails during shopping, another server continues the session.

## Fault Tolerance in Microservices

Consider this e-commerce service graph:

```text
                        API Gateway
                                     |
       -------------------------
       |      |        |      |
 Order  Payment Inventory User
```

If Inventory fails:

- Order service can queue requests.
- Payment service can continue processing.
- Inventory service can recover and process queued updates later.

This prevents one service failure from taking down the entire application.